<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19868384</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2128218</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>A METHOD OF STANDARDIZING BACTERIAL SUSPENSIONS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Gates</surname><given-names>Frederick L.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1cf1180N0x3cb0db0">From the Laboratories of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>1</month><year>1920</year></pub-date><volume>31</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>105</fpage><lpage>114</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>10</month><year>1919</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1920, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The opacity of a bacterial suspension is measured by the length of the column of the suspension required to cause the disappearance of a wire loop. By a simple formula the measured opacity is translated into terms of the concentration of bacteria per cc., and so made comparable with that of other suspensions of the same organism. An instrument for measuring the opacity of bacterial suspensions is described in detail. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>